# 36. What's Next?

The next step is to implement Control and Status Registers (CSRs) and begin supporting the RISC-V Privileged Architecture.

The current RV32IMA pipeline implements the base instruction execution environment, but operating systems such as Linux require additional processor functionality for:

- Exceptions
- Interrupts
- Privilege levels
- Trap handling
- System control
- Operating-system interaction

The next project will therefore introduce the CSR system and the first parts of the RISC-V Privileged Architecture.

The main goals are:

- Implement CSR instructions:
  - "CSRRW"
  - "CSRRS"
  - "CSRRC"
  - "CSRRWI"
  - "CSRRSI"
  - "CSRRCI"
- Implement a CSR register file
- Implement CSR address decoding
- Implement CSR read/write permissions
- Add the "M" (Machine) privilege mode
- Implement important machine-level CSRs
- Implement exception and trap handling
- Implement "mstatus"
- Implement "mtvec"
- Implement "mepc"
- Implement "mcause"
- Implement "mtval"
- Implement "mie"
- Implement "mip"
- Connect traps and interrupts to the pipeline
- Handle "ECALL", "EBREAK", and illegal instructions
- Implement "MRET"
- Flush the pipeline correctly when entering and returning from traps
- Verify CSR and trap behavior through simulation

The goal is not to implement the complete RISC-V Privileged Architecture immediately. Instead, the implementation will start with the Machine mode functionality required to establish a proper trap and interrupt system.

This will provide an important foundation for later operating-system support and the eventual Linux-compatible SoC.

The planned execution environment will therefore evolve from:

RV32IMA
    ↓
RV32IMA + 5-stage pipeline
    ↓
RV32IMA + CSR support
    ↓
Machine-mode privileged architecture
    ↓
Exceptions & interrupts
    ↓
Operating-system support
    ↓
Linux-compatible SoC


A future step can expand this further with additional privilege modes and privileged features as required by the target software environment.
